archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2255772Z Note: a multi-user installation is possible. See https://nix.dev/manual/nix/stable/installation/installing-binary.html#multi-user-installation
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2267540Z ^[[1;31mSwitching to the Multi-user Installer^[[0m
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2372157Z ^[[32mWelcome to the Multi-User Nix Installation
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2382956Z ^[[0m
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2383810Z This installation tool will set up your computer with the Nix package
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2384750Z manager. This will happen in a few stages:
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2385197Z 
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2386120Z 1. Make sure your computer doesn't already have Nix. If it does, I
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2387136Z    will show you instructions on how to clean up your old install.
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2387724Z 
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2388094Z 2. Show you what I am going to install and where. Then I will ask
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2388858Z    if you are ready to continue.
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2389249Z 
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2389987Z 3. Create the system users ^[[34m(uids [30001..30008])^[[0m and groups ^[[34m(gid 30000)^[[0m
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2392057Z    that the Nix daemon uses to run builds. To create system users
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2392683Z    in a different range, exit and run this tool again with
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2393284Z    NIX_FIRST_BUILD_UID set.
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2393621Z 
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2394491Z 4. Perform the basic installation of the Nix files daemon.
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2396274Z 
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2396898Z 5. Configure your shell to import special Nix Profile files, so you
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2397628Z    can use Nix.
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2397876Z 
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2398050Z 6. Start the Nix daemon.
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2398237Z 
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2398694Z ^[[32m^[[4;32mWould you like to see a more detailed list of what I will do?
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2399196Z ^[[0mNo TTY, assuming you would say yes :)
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2399426Z 
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2399531Z I will:
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2399761Z 
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2399970Z  - make sure your computer doesn't already have Nix files
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2400402Z    (if it does, I will tell you how to clean them up.)
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2400855Z  - create local users (see the list above for the users I'll make)
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2401830Z  - create a local group (nixbld)
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2402219Z  - install Nix in /nix
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2402549Z  - create a configuration file in /etc/nix
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2402991Z  - set up the "default profile" by creating some Nix-related files in
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2403437Z    /root
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2414829Z  - back up /etc/bash.bashrc to /etc/bash.bashrc.backup-before-nix
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2415700Z  - update /etc/bash.bashrc to include some Nix configuration
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2426896Z  - load and start a service (at /etc/systemd/system/nix-daemon.service
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2427588Z    and /etc/systemd/system/nix-daemon.socket) for nix-daemon
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2428095Z 
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2429353Z ^[[32m^[[4;32mReady to continue?
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2430048Z ^[[0mNo TTY, assuming you would say yes :)
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2448896Z 
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2449502Z ^[[34m---- let's talk about sudo -----------------------------------------------------
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2460399Z ^[[0mThis script is going to call sudo a lot. Normally, it would show you
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2461339Z exactly what commands it is running and why. However, the script is
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2462154Z run in a headless fashion, like this:
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2462544Z 
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2462870Z   $ curl -L https://nixos.org/nix/install | sh
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2463334Z 
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2463728Z or maybe in a CI pipeline. Because of that, I'm going to skip the
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2464538Z verbose output in the interest of brevity.
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2464985Z 
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2465203Z If you would like to
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2465660Z see the output, try like this:
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2465870Z 
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2466136Z   $ curl -L -o install-nix https://nixos.org/nix/install
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2466592Z   $ sh ./install-nix
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2466766Z 
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2466774Z 
archive	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-22T15:08:43.2467039Z ^[[32m~~> Checking for artifacts of previous installs
